e to be given over at least 1 minute, then 50 mg after 5 minutes if required, maximum 200 mg per course. By IV infusion: Initially 2 mg/min until a satisfactory response is achieved, then discontinue; usual dose 50–200 mg. Hypertension following MI, by IV infusion: 15 mg/hour,'),(369,599,'ATC code: C07AB Tablet (e/r), 25mg, 50mg, LoU 3 Hypertension Initially 100 mg daily, increased if necessary to 200 mg daily in 1–2 divided doses, high doses are rarely required. Arrhythmias: Usual dose 50 mg 2–3 times a day, then increased if necessary up to 300 mg daily in divided doses.'),(370,600,'ATC code: C07AB12 Tablet, 2.5 mg, 5 mg LOU 3 Essential hypertension: 5 mg daily may be increased every 2 weeks; not to exceed 40 mg/day. Elderly: Initially 2.5 mg once daily, then increased if necessary to 5 mg once daily Hypertension in patient with renal impairment: Initially 2.5 mg once daily, then increased if necessary to 5 mg once daily.'),(371,601,'ATC code: C08CA01 Tablet, 5 mg, 10 mg, LOU 3 Angina, oral: Initially 5 mg once daily, increased if necessary; maximum 10 mg once daily Hypertension, oral: Initially 5 mg once daily, increased if necessary; maximum 10 mg once daily'),(372,605,'ATC code: C08CA05 Tablet (slow release), 20 mg, LOU 3'),(373,606,'ATC code: C03BA04 Tablet, 12.5 mg, LOU 4'),(374,607,'ATC code: C03AA03 Tablet (scored), 25 mg, LOU 3 Heart failure, oral: Initially 25 mg daily on rising, increased to 50 mg daily, if necessary » Elderly: Initially 12.5 mg daily on rising Hypertension, oral: 12.5 mg daily, increased to 25–50 mg daily if necessary. Oedema, oral: Initially 25 mg daily on rising, increased to 50 mg daily if necessary'),(375,608,'ATC code: C02AB02 (racemic) Tablet (f/c), 250 mg, 500 mg, LOU 4 Hypertension in pregnancy, oral: Initially 250 mg 2–3 times daily, gradually increased at intervals of 2 or more days, if necessary; maximum 3 g daily Paediatric: No or insufficient experience in children and adolescents, therefore its use is not recommended. Contraindications: depression, active liver disease,'),(376,609,'ATC code: C03DA01 Tablet (scored), 25 mg, LOU 4 Oedema, oral: 100–200 mg daily, increase if necessary to 400 mg daily in resistant oedema; usual maintenance dose, 25–200 mg daily Primary hyperaldosteronism, oral: 400 mg daily for 3–4 weeks Preoperative management, oral: 100–400 mg daily, if not suitable for surgery, give the lowest effective dose'),(377,610,'ATC code: C02DB02 Tablet (scored), 25 mg (as HCl), LOU 3 Tablet (scored), 50 mg (as HCl), LOU 3 277 14 Moderate to severe hypertension (adjunct), oral: Initially 25 mg twice daily, increase if necessary up to 50 mg twice daily Heart failure (with long-acting nitrate) (initiated in hospital or under specialist supervision), oral: Initially 25 mg 3–4 times a day, subsequent doses to be increased every 2 days if necessary; usual maintenance 50–75'),(378,620,'ATC code: P01BC02 Tablet, 250 mg (as HCl), LOU 4 Prophylaxis of malaria, oral: 250 mg once a week'),(379,621,'Tablet, 1.5 mg. Adult: Essential hypertension: 1.5 mg daily, preferably in the morning; may increase at 4-week intervals up to 5 mg. Paediatric: Not recommended.'),(380,622,'Tablet, 2 mg, LOU 4. Adult: Hypertension, oral: Initially 1 mg once daily for 1–2 weeks, then increased to 2 mg once daily, then increased if necessary to 4 mg once daily; maximum 16 mg per day. Paediatric: No or insufficient experience in children and adolescents; use is not recommended.'),(381,623,'Capsule, 500 micrograms, 1 mg, 5 mg, LOU 4. Adult: Hypertension, oral: Initially 500 micrograms 2–3 times a day for 3–7 days; initial dose at bedtime, then 1 mg 2–3 times a day for a further 3–7 days, then increased if necessary up to 20 mg daily in divided doses. Congestive heart failure: Initially 500 micrograms 2–4 times a day, then increased to 4 mg daily in divided doses; maintenance 4–20 mg daily. Benign prostatic hyperplasia: Initially 500 micrograms twice daily for 3–7 days; adjust according to response; maintenance 2 mg twice daily. Elderly: initiate with lowest possible dose. Paediatric hypertension: Child 1 month–11 years initially 10–15 micrograms/kg 2–4 times a day, initial dose at bedtime, then increased gradually, maximum 20 mg per day. Child 12–17 years: initially 500 micrograms 2–3 times a day for 3–7 days, then 1 mg 2–3 times a day, then up to 20 mg daily in divided doses. Paediatric congestive heart failure: Child 1 month–11 years initially 5 micrograms/kg twice daily, then increased to 100 micrograms/kg daily in divided doses.'),(382,624,'Capsule, 10 mg, LOU 5. Adult: Hypertension in phaeochromocytoma, oral: Initially 10 mg daily, increased in steps of 10 mg daily until hypertension is controlled or treatment is not tolerated; maintenance 1–2 mg/kg daily in 2 divided doses. Elderly: 10 mg dose should be sufficient. Paediatric: 0.5–1 mg/kg twice daily, adjusted according to response.'),(383,625,'Tablet, film coated, 62.5 mg, LOU 4. Adult pulmonary arterial hypertension: >40 kg, start 62.5 mg twice daily for 4 weeks, then increase to 125 mg twice daily; maximum 250 mg per dose and 500 mg per day. <40 kg: maintain 62.5 mg twice daily. On discontinuation, consider reducing to 62.5 mg twice daily for 3–7 days. Paediatric: From 12 years and <40 kg, 62.5 mg twice daily. Below 12 years, dose is based on body weight and must be determined by a specialist.'),(384,626,'Tablet, 25 mg, LOU 4. Adult pulmonary arterial hypertension, oral: 20 mg 3 times a day. Paediatric pulmonary arterial hypertension: Neonate and child 1–11 months, initially 250–500 micrograms/kg every 4–8 hours, adjusted according to response; maximum 30 mg per day. Child 1–17 years up to 20 kg: 10 mg 3 times a day. Child 1–17 years 20 kg and above: 20 mg 3 times a day.'),(385,627,'Tablet, 20 mg, LOU 4. Adult pulmonary arterial hypertension, initiated under specialist supervision: 40 mg once daily. Paediatric: Safety and efficacy not established.'),(386,628,'Injection solution, 12.5 mg/mL, 20 mL. Adult: Inotropic support in infarction, cardiac surgery, cardiomyopathies, septic shock, cardiogenic shock and during positive end expiratory pressure ventilation, by IV infusion: usual dose 2.5–10 micrograms/kg/min, adjusted according to response; alternatively 0.5–40 micrograms/kg/min. Paediatric: Neonate initially 5 micrograms/kg/min, adjusted to 2–20 micrograms/kg/min; doses as low as 0.5–1 microgram/kg/min have been used. Child initially 5 micrograms/kg/min, adjusted to 2–20 micrograms/kg/min; doses as low as 0.5–1 microgram/kg/min have been used.'),(387,629,'Injection, 40 mg/mL as HCl, 5 mL vial. Adult cardiogenic shock: by infusion into a large vein, initially 2–5 micrograms/kg/min, gradually increased by 5–10 micrograms/kg/min according to blood pressure, cardiac output and urine output; seriously ill patients may require up to 20–50 micrograms/kg/min. Paediatric: Neonate initially 3 micrograms/kg/min, maximum 20 micrograms/kg/min; child initially 5 micrograms/kg/min, maximum 20 micrograms/kg/min, adjusted according to response.'),(388,630,'Tablet, 10 mg. Adult symptomatic chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (18–84 years): 10 mg once daily. Paediatric safety and efficacy not established for use in heart failure.'),(389,631,'Tablet, 25 mg. Adult: Following myocardial infarction with left ventricular ejection fraction below 40% and evidence of heart failure, start 25 mg daily, then increase to 50 mg daily within 4 weeks. Chronic mild heart failure with left ventricular ejection fraction below 30%: start 25 mg daily, then increase to 50 mg daily within 4 weeks. Hypertension: initial 50 mg per day. Paediatric safety and efficacy not established. Renal impairment: initially 25 mg on alternate days if creatinine clearance is 30–60 mL/minute; avoid if below 30 mL/minute.'),(390,632,'Injection, 10 mg/mL (2 mL amp), tablet 40 mg, oral liquid 20 mg/5 mL. Adult heart-failure oedema, oral: 40 mg stat in the morning; maintenance 20 mg once daily or 40 mg on alternate days. By IM injection or slow IV injection/infusion: initially 20–50 mg, then increase in steps of 20 mg every 2 hours if required; doses above 50 mg by IV infusion only; maximum 1.5 g per day. Paediatric: Neonate oral 0.5–2 mg/kg every 12–24 hours; child 1 month–11 years 0.5–2 mg/kg 2–3 times daily, maximum 12 mg/kg (80 mg) daily; child 12–17 years 20–40 mg daily, increased to 80–120 mg daily in resistant oedema.'),(391,633,'Tablet, 5 mg. Adult: Oedema in congestive heart failure, oedema in renal disease, hypertension: initially 2.5 mg daily, increased if necessary to 5 mg daily. Paediatric: Not recommended.'),(392,634,'Injection (solution), 1 mg/mL (10 mL). Short-term treatment of severe congestive heart failure unresponsive to conventional maintenance therapy, acute heart failure including low-output states following heart surgery: IV initially 50 micrograms/kg over 10 minutes, followed by IV infusion 375–750 nanograms/kg/min; maximum 1.13 mg/kg/day. Paediatric congestive heart failure/low cardiac output following cardiac surgery/shock: neonate initially 50–75 micrograms/kg over 30–60 minutes, then continuous IV infusion 30–45 micrograms/kg/hour for 2–3 days.'),(393,635,'Injection, 2.5 mg/mL (10 mL amp). Adult: Unresponsive congestive heart failure including secondary to acute MI, acute left-sided heart failure and acute MI: IV infusion 20–25 micrograms/min, decreased to 10 micrograms/min or increased in steps of 20–25 micrograms/min every 15–30 minutes until desired effect. Refractory unstable angina/coronary insufficiency: IV infusion 10 micrograms/min, increased by 10 micrograms/min at about 30-minute intervals. Hypertensive episodes/myocardial ischaemia during and after cardiac surgery: IV infusion 15–20 micrograms/min, with subsequent increments of 10–15 micrograms/min.'),(394,636,'Tablet (scored), 10 mg and 20 mg. Adult: Oedema, oral: 5 mg once daily, preferably in the morning, then increase if necessary to 20 mg once daily; maximum 40 mg/day. Paediatric: insufficient experience; not recommended unless under specialist supervision.'),(395,637,'Tablet, 75 mg. Adult: Prevention of atherothrombotic events in percutaneous coronary intervention (with aspirin), oral: loading dose 300 mg before the procedure; alternatively 600 mg. Peripheral arterial disease or within 35 days of MI or within 6 months of ischaemic stroke: 75 mg once daily. Acute coronary syndrome without ST-segment elevation (with aspirin): initially 300 mg, then 75 mg daily for up to 12 months. Acute MI with ST-segment elevation (with aspirin): age 18–75 years initially 300 mg, then 75 mg for at least 4 weeks; age 76 years and over: 75 mg daily for at least 4 weeks.'),(396,638,'Powder for injection, 50 mg. Adult acute myocardial infarction: IV injection 10 mg within 6–12 hours of symptom onset, followed by IV infusion 50 mg over 60 minutes, then 10 mg for 4 infusions over 30 minutes each; total 100 mg over 3 hours; maximum 1.5 mg/kg in patients below 65 kg. Accelerated regimen: body weight up to 65 kg, initially 15 mg within 6 hours, then 0.75 mg/kg over 30 minutes followed by 0.5 mg/kg over 60 minutes; maximum 100 mg over 90 minutes. Body weight 65 kg and above: initially 15 mg, then 50 mg over 30 minutes followed by 35 mg over 60 minutes; maximum 100 mg over 90 minutes.'),(397,639,'Solution, 2%. Disinfection of clean instruments: completely immerse in undiluted 2% solution for 20 minutes at 20 °C; up to 3 hours may be required for certain instruments such as bronchoscopes with possible mycobacterial contamination. Sterilization of clean instruments: immerse in undiluted solution for up to 10 hours; rinse with sterile water or ethanol.'),(398,640,'Tablet (dispersible), 20 mg. Paediatric: Adjunct to oral rehydration therapy in acute diarrhoea. Infant under 6 months: 10 mg elemental zinc daily for 10–14 days. Infant or child over 6 months: 20 mg elemental zinc daily for 10–14 days.'),(399,641,'Injection, 1 mg as acetate in 8.5 mL solution. Adult: Treatment of bleeding oesophageal varices: initially 1–2 mg IV over 1 minute; weight <50 kg: 1 mg, 50–70 kg: 1.5 mg, >70 kg: 2 mg. After the initial injection, reduce to 1 mg every 4–6 hours. Maximum daily dose 120 micrograms/kg. Treatment limited to 2–3 days.'),(400,642,'Solution for injection, 20 units/mL. Adult: Management of ascites and gastroesophageal variceal hemorrhage; initial control of oesophageal variceal bleeding: continuous infusion 0.2–0.4 units/min, maximum 0.8 units/min. Paediatric: initially 0.3 unit/kg over 20–30 minutes, then 0.3 unit/kg/hour adjusted according to response.'),(401,643,'Tablet, 100 micrograms (0.1 mg) as acetate. Adult: Adrenocortical insufficiency/Addison’s disease and orthostatic hypotension: 0.1–0.2 mg/day; Addison’s disease initially 0.1 mg/day, reduce to 0.05 mg/day if transient hypertension develops; salt-losing adrenogenital syndrome 0.05–0.2 mg/day in 1–2 divided doses. Paediatric: 50–100 micrograms daily for adrenal insufficiency/Addison’s disease; congenital adrenal hyperplasia 50–300 micrograms/day, adjusted according to response.'),(402,644,'Tablet, 0.3 mg; vaginal cream, 0.625 mg/g. Adult: Atrophic vaginitis, vaginal cream 1–2 g daily cyclically, maximum 4 g/day; oral initial 0.3 mg/day. Menopausal/postmenopausal symptoms: 0.3–1.25 mg daily with a cyclic progestogen. Postmenopausal osteoporosis prophylaxis: initial 0.3 mg daily. Primary ovarian failure: 1.25 mg daily. Female hypogonadism: 0.3 or 0.625 mg daily cyclically. Paediatric safety and efficacy not established.'),(403,645,'Transdermal patch, 0.1 mg/day (100 micrograms in 24 hours). Adult: Hormone replacement therapy for oestrogen deficiency symptoms in postmenopausal women and prevention of osteoporosis in high-risk postmenopausal women: apply patch twice weekly (every 3–4 days); initiate and continue at the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration.'),(404,646,'Tablet, 5 mg. Adult: Abnormal uterine bleeding due to hormonal imbalance: 5–10 mg daily for 5–10 days beginning on day 16 or 21 of the menstrual cycle; alternatively 10 mg daily for 10 days beginning on day 16. Secondary physiologic amenorrhea: 5–10 mg daily for 5–10 days; alternatively 10 mg for 10 days.'),(405,647,'Tablet, modified-release 30 mg or 60 mg; immediate-release 40 mg or 80 mg. Adult type 2 diabetes: modified-release initially 30 mg once daily, adjusted according to response up to 120 mg once daily; immediate-release initially 40–80 mg daily, adjusted up to 160 mg as a single dose, higher doses divided, maximum 320 mg daily.'),(406,648,'Tablet, 500 mg, 850 mg, 1 g as hydrochloride. Adult type 2 diabetes: 500 mg every 8 hours with or after food; maximum 2 g daily in divided doses. eGFR 30–44 mL/min: maximum 1000 mg/day; eGFR below 30 mL/min: discontinue.'),(407,649,'Tablet, 15 mg or 30 mg. Adult type 2 diabetes: monotherapy initially 15–30 mg once daily; if response inadequate, increase in increments up to 45 mg once daily; maximum 45 mg once daily. Combination therapy maximum 45 mg/day.'),(408,650,'Tablets, 50 mg or 100 mg. Adult type 2 diabetes: monotherapy 100 mg once daily. In combination therapy with metformin and/or thiazolidinedione, continue the existing dose of the other medicine and administer sitagliptin concomitantly.'),(409,651,'Suspension, 50 mg/mL. Adult: Hypoglycaemia due to hyperinsulinism: 5 mg/kg divided into 2 or 3 doses per 24 hours; may be increased according to response. Usual maintenance dose 3–8 mg/kg/day in 2–3 divided doses.'),(410,652,'Injection, 1 mg/mL. Adult diabetic hypoglycaemia: 1 mg by SC or IM injection; if no response within 10 minutes, give intravenous dextrose. Paediatric: child 1 month–8 years and up to 25 kg: 500 micrograms SC or IM; child 9–17 years and 25 kg or above: 1 mg SC or IM.'),(411,653,'Tablet, 5 mg or 10 mg. Adult: Hyperthyroidism and control of thyrotoxicosis: 15–40 mg daily in divided doses until euthyroid, usually 4–8 weeks; then maintenance 5–15 mg daily for 12–18 months. Paediatric: Neonate initially 750 micrograms/kg daily until euthyroid, usually 8–12 weeks, then gradually reduce to 30–60% of the initial dose.'),(412,654,'Solution, approximately 130 mg total iodine/mL. Adult thyrotoxicosis/thyrotoxic crisis: 0.1–0.3 mL three times daily. Paediatric neonatal thyrotoxicosis: 0.05–0.1 mL three times daily; preoperative thyrotoxicosis: 0.1–0.3 mL three times daily; thyrotoxic crisis: infant 0.2–0.3 mL three times daily, child 1 mL three times daily.'),(413,655,'Tablet, 50 mg. Adult thyrotoxic crisis/storm: loading dose 500–1000 mg followed by 250 mg every 4 hours. Hyperthyroidism including Graves disease: initially 50–150 mg three times daily until euthyroid; maintenance 50 mg 2–3 times daily for 12–18 months, then discontinue if thyroid function is normal.'),(414,656,'Tablet, 30 mg. Adult secondary hyperparathyroidism in end-stage renal disease on haemodialysis: initially 30 mg once daily, adjust every 2–4 weeks according to response; maximum 180 mg/day. Severe hypercalcaemia in primary hyperparathyroidism: 30 mg twice daily, maximum 90 mg four times daily, adjusted every 2–4 weeks. Paediatric 3–17 years: initially 0.5 mg/kg/day, titrated every 4 weeks.'),(415,657,'Oral tablet, 0.5 mg. Adult prevention of lactation: 1 mg as a single dose on the first day postpartum. Suppression of established lactation: 250 micrograms every 12 hours for 2 days. Hyperprolactinaemic disorders: initially 500 micrograms once weekly, increased in steps of 500 micrograms every month; usual dose 0.25–2 mg once weekly; maximum 4.5 mg/week. Acromegaly/Cushing disease: 0.25–0.5 mg twice weekly, maximum 7 mg/week.'),(416,658,'Injection, 4 micrograms/mL; nasal spray, 10 micrograms/spray. Adult central diabetes insipidus: injection 1–4 micrograms daily; intranasal spray 10–40 micrograms daily in 1–2 divided doses. Paediatric: neonate initially 100 nanograms once daily by IM injection; child 1 month–11 years initially 400 nanograms once daily by SC/IM injection; child 12–17 years initially 1–4 micrograms once daily. Intranasal: child 1–23 months 2.5–5 micrograms 1–2 times daily; child 2–11 years 5–20 micrograms 1–2 times daily; child 12–17 years 10–20 micrograms 1–2 times daily.'),(417,659,'Eye drops, 0.3% as sulphate. Adult: Conjunctivitis: instill 1–2 drops in affected eye(s) every 2–4 hours for the first 2 days, then 4 times daily for an additional 5 days. Corneal ulcer: 1–2 drops every 30 minutes while awake and every 4–6 hours after retiring for the first 2 days; from day 3, 1–2 drops every hour while awake for 4–6 days, then 1–2 drops 4 times daily until clinical cure. Paediatric: for conjunctivitis in children over 1 year, 1–2 drops every 2–4 hours for the first 2 days, then 4 times daily for 5 days.'),(418,660,'Eye drops, 0.3% as sulphate, 5 mL. Adult: Treatment of external infections of the eye and its adnexa caused by susceptible bacteria: apply twice daily for 6–8 days. Severe infection: apply 4 times daily for the first day, then twice daily for 5–7 days. Paediatric 1–17 years: simple infections twice daily for 6–8 days; severe infection 4 times on the first day, then twice daily for 5–7 days.'),(419,661,'Implant, 150 mg (2 × 75 mg rods). Adult contraception: insert 2 implants of 75 mg each subdermally in the non-dominant upper arm within the first 7 days of the menstrual cycle or immediately following first- or second-trimester abortion or delivery. Replace after 5 years.'),(420,662,'Injection, 5,000 IU/vial. Adult: Superovulation for assisted conception, SC: 5,000–10,000 IU one day following the last dose of menotropins. Male hypogonadism, IM: 500–1,000 IU three times weekly for 3 weeks, then 500–1,000 IU twice weekly for 3 weeks; alternative regimen 4,000 IU three times weekly for 6–9 months, then 2,000 IU three times weekly for 3 months.'),(421,663,'Injection, 75 IU. Adult ovulation induction: 150 IU daily for 5 days; subsequent doses based on patient response, with dose adjustments no more often than every 2 days and by no more than 75–150 IU; maximum 450 IU/day and maximum treatment duration 12 days. Assisted reproductive technologies: initially 225 IU once daily from cycle day 2 or 3; adjust after 5 days based on ovarian response; maximum 450 IU/day.'),(422,129,'Tablet, 50 mg (as citrate). Adult: Anovulatory infertility, oral: 50 mg daily for 5 days, starting within 5 days of onset of menstruation, preferably on the second day, or at any time if cycles have ceased; a second course of 100 mg daily for 5 days may be given in the absence of ovulation; discontinue if ovulation does not occur after 3 courses of treatment, or if 3 ovulatory responses occur but pregnancy is not achieved.'),(423,94,'Injection (depot, SC), 3.75 mg (as acetate). Adult: Reduce size of uterine fibroids and associated bleeding before surgery, by SC or IM injection: 3.75 mg every month, usually for 3–4 months; maximum 6 months.'),(424,112,'Solution for injection, 0.5 mg/mL. Adult: Uncomplicated premature labour between 22 and 37 weeks, under specialist hospital supervision, by IV infusion: initially 5 micrograms/minute for 20 minutes, then increase in steps of 2.5 micrograms/minute every 20 minutes until contractions cease; more than 10 micrograms/minute should seldom be given and 20 micrograms/minute should not be exceeded. Continue for 1 hour, then reduce in steps of 2.5 micrograms/minute every 20 minutes to the lowest dose maintaining suppression; maximum total duration 48 hours.'),(425,664,'Sublingual combination with naloxone. Adult maintenance treatment of opioid dependence: a single daily dose adjusted in increments/decrements of 2 mg/0.5 mg or 4 mg/1 mg buprenorphine/naloxone, to a maximum of 16 mg/4 mg buprenorphine/naloxone per day.'),(426,665,'Tablet, 70 mg. Adult: Postmenopausal osteoporosis: 70 mg once weekly. Osteoporosis in men: 70 mg once weekly. Prevention and treatment of corticosteroid-induced osteoporosis in postmenopausal women not receiving hormone replacement therapy: 70 mg once weekly. Paediatric osteogenesis imperfecta/bone manifestations in Gaucher disease: child ≤30 kg, 35 mg once weekly; child 30 to <40 kg, 35 or 70 mg once weekly; child ≥40 kg, 70 mg once weekly.'),(427,666,'Ointment, 521,700 IU + 100 mg (15 g). Adult and paediatric: Enzymatic debridement of large burn wounds and bed sores: apply ointment directly to the wound, cover with appropriate dressing and secure in place; if possible, change dressing 1–2 times per day.'),(428,667,'Gel (water-based), 15 g. Adult and paediatric: Assists in epithelialization of wounds, topical: apply once or twice after showering.'),(429,668,'Powder for oral dilution to make 1 L (42 g), WHO formula. Paediatric: Rehydration and correction of electrolyte derangements in severe acute malnutrition: ReSoMal 5 mL/kg every 30 minutes for 2 hours, orally or by nasogastric tube, then 5–10 mL/kg/hour for the next 4–10 hours. For prevention of dehydration with continuing watery diarrhoea, replace approximate stool losses with ReSoMal; as a guide give 50–100 mL after each watery stool.'),(430,669,'Oral solution, 500 mg + 334 mg/5 mL (30 mL). Adult: Urine alkalinization, prevention of nephrolithiasis and management of metabolic acidosis: 10–30 mL diluted in up to 170 mL water/juice after meals and at bedtime as needed. Paediatric: children 2 years and older 5–15 mL diluted in 30–90 mL water/juice after meals and at bedtime as needed.'),(431,670,'5% isotonic (500 mL), 10% hypertonic (500 mL), and 50% hypertonic (50 mL). Adult fluid replacement: dose determined according to clinical status and electrolyte monitoring. Adult hypoglycaemia: 50% glucose solution IV into a large vein, 25 mL. Neonatal hypoglycaemia: 10% glucose 2.5 mL/kg IV over 5 minutes, then 5 mL/kg/hour. Infant or child hypoglycaemia: 10% glucose 5 mL/kg IV as a bolus.'),(432,671,'Injectable solution, glucose 5% + sodium chloride 0.9%. Adult: Fluid replacement by IV infusion, dose determined on the basis of clinical and, whenever possible, electrolyte monitoring. Paediatric: Fluid replacement by IV infusion; neonate, infant or child, dose determined on the basis of clinical and, whenever possible, electrolyte monitoring.'),(433,672,'Sterile aqueous solution for injection, potassium acid phosphate 13.6% w/v. Adult: Electrolyte replacement therapy for potassium and phosphate ions, by slow IV infusion: equivalent of 10 mmol (310 mg) phosphorus daily; dilute before use. Paediatric: equivalent of 1.5–2.0 mmol (46.5–62 mg) phosphorus daily; dilute before use.'),(434,673,'Injectable solution for infusion, BP formula, 500 mL. Adult: preoperative and perioperative fluid and electrolyte replacement; hypovolaemic shock. Metabolic acidosis: by slow IV injection of a strong solution up to 8.4% or by continuous IV infusion of a weaker solution, usually 1.4%, in an amount appropriate to the body base deficit. Paediatric: fluid and electrolyte replacement or hypovolaemic shock by IV infusion, dose determined according to clinical and electrolyte monitoring.'),(435,674,'Injection, 10 mL ampoule. Adult and paediatric: used in preparations intended for parenteral administration and other sterile preparations, for dissolving or diluting agents for parenteral administration. Dosage is the amount required to dissolve or dilute the other agent. Use aseptic technique.'),(436,675,'Tablet, 50 mg, 250 mg or 1 g. Adult treatment of scurvy: 1–2 g orally for the first 2 days, then 500 mg daily for a week; alternative 250 mg four times daily for a week. Adult prophylaxis: males 90 mg/day, females 75 mg/day; pregnancy 85 mg/day. Paediatric treatment of scurvy: not less than 250 mg daily in 1–2 divided doses until clinical signs disappear. Paediatric prophylaxis: 25–75 mg daily.'),(437,676,'Capsule, 250 micrograms; injection, 1 microgram/mL. Adult hypocalcaemia due to hypoparathyroidism: oral initially 0.25 micrograms/day in the morning, increase at 2–4 week intervals; usual dose 0.5–2 micrograms once daily. Chronic renal dialysis: initially 0.25 micrograms daily or every other day; adjust according to response. Paediatric hypoparathyroidism: age 1–5 years 0.25 micrograms/day, usual 0.25–0.75 micrograms/day; age 6 years and older initially 0.25 micrograms/day, usual 0.5–2 micrograms/day.'),(438,677,'Tablet, 500 mg. Adult: Adjunctive therapy in osteoporosis 2–3 tablets daily; prevention and treatment of calcium deficiency 2–3 tablets daily; phosphate binder, dose according to individual serum phosphate level. Paediatric phosphate binding in renal failure/hyperphosphataemia: doses adjusted by age and response; calcium deficiency doses also adjusted according to response.'),(439,678,'Oral liquid drops, 400 IU/mL; injection 300,000 IU/1 mL. Adult nutritional supplementation: 600 IU (15 micrograms) daily; pregnancy/lactation 600 IU daily. Osteoporosis prophylaxis/treatment in adults over 50 years: 800–1000 IU (20–25 micrograms) once daily with calcium supplements. Further dosing is individualised according to indication and clinical response.'),(440,679,'Oral liquid 250 micrograms (10,000 IU)/mL; oral solution 200 IU/drop; tablet/capsule 250 micrograms (10,000 IU) or 1.25 mg (50,000 IU). Adult vitamin D deficiency: 50,000 IU once weekly for 8 weeks or 6000 IU once daily for 8 weeks, followed by maintenance 1500–2000 IU/day. Hypoparathyroidism: 50,000–200,000 IU once daily plus calcium. Other indications require individualised dosing.'),(441,680,'Tablet, 500 mg. Adult pellagra: 300–500 mg/day divided into 2–3 doses. Paediatric pellagra: 100–300 mg/day divided into 2–3 doses.'),(442,681,'Tablet/capsule, 1 g; liquid, 250–500 mg/100 mL. Adult hypertriglyceridaemia and omega-3 deficiency in cardiovascular patients: 4 g once daily or 2 g twice daily. Paediatric: safety and efficacy not established below 18 years.'),(443,682,'Tablet, 25 mg or scored 50 mg as hydrochloride. Adult pyridoxine deficiency: 2.5–10 mg/day orally. Nausea in pregnancy: 10–25 mg every 8 hours. Prevention of peripheral neuropathy in patients receiving isoniazid for tuberculosis: 25–50 mg/day. Paediatric metabolic disorders: neonate 50–100 mg 1–2 times daily; infant or child 50–250 mg 1–2 times daily. Isoniazid-induced neuropathy: neonate 5–10 mg daily; infant or child 10–20 mg 2–3 times daily.'),(444,683,'Capsule, 50,000 IU, 100,000 IU or 200,000 IU as palmitate. Adult vitamin A deficiency prophylaxis: 50,000 IU once daily. Xerophthalmia: 200,000 IU once daily for 2 days, repeated after 2 weeks, with specified lower doses for females of reproductive age. Paediatric prevention: under 6 months 50,000 IU single dose; 6–12 months 100,000 IU every 4–6 months; over 12 months 200,000 IU every 4–6 months. Treatment of xerophthalmia is age-specific.'),(445,684,'IV infusion. Adult suspected or established Wernicke encephalopathy: 2–3 pairs three times daily for 3–5 days, followed by one pair once daily for a further 3–5 days or while improvement continues. Alcohol withdrawal syndrome, prevention of Wernicke encephalopathy and severe sepsis/septic shock: 1 pair every 12 hours over 30 minutes for 4 days or until ICU discharge. Paediatric doses not listed for these indications.'),(446,685,'Tablet, 500 micrograms; injection, 1000 micrograms/mL. Adult cobalamin deficiency treatment: 500 micrograms daily orally. Prophylaxis of cobalamin deficiency: 1000 micrograms once daily orally.'),(447,686,'Parenteral nutrition dosing is individualized. Adult energy requirement 25–30 kcal/kg/day; protein 0.8–2 g/kg/day; lipid 0.7–1.5 g/kg/day; fish oil 0.1–0.2 g/kg/day. Paediatric requirements are based on age and weight.'),(448,687,'Solution for IV infusion, 1 L. Used for pancreatic failure and hepatic disease and for short-term parenteral nutrition when enteral nutrition is contraindicated or inadequate. Adult dose individualized; upper limit 40 mL/kg/day. Paediatric dose depends on weight and age. Administer as slow continuous IV infusion via central line over 12–24 hours.'),(449,688,'Solution for IV infusion, 1 L and 2 L. Used for long-term parenteral nutrition where the gastrointestinal tract is non-functional or in short bowel syndrome and a central venous catheter is present. Adult dose individualized; do not exceed 40 mL/kg/day. Not recommended below 2 years. Administer as slow continuous IV infusion via central line over 12–24 hours.'),(450,689,'20% fat emulsion for IV infusion, 100 mL and 500 mL. Adult standard dose: 1–2 g fat/kg/day, corresponding to 5–10 mL/kg/day. To prevent fatty-acid deficiency, 500 mL of 10% lipids once or twice weekly. Recommended infusion rate 0.125 g fat/kg/hour; maximum 2.5 g fat/kg/day.'),(451,690,'Lipid-soluble vitamins for IV infusion for adults, 10 mL, and paediatric use, 10 mL. Intended as a supplement in complete IV nutrition to meet daily requirements of fat-soluble vitamins. Adult: based on expert opinion and general recommendations; should be given daily except vitamin K, which should be given weekly parenterally or intramuscularly at 2–4 mg/week depending on prothrombin time. Paediatric dosing is based on age and weight according to parenteral vitamin recommendations.'),(452,691,'Water-soluble vitamins for IV infusion for adults and children, 10 mL. Intended as a supplement in IV nutrition to meet daily requirements. Adults: individualized based on need. Children and infants weighing less than 10 kg: 1 mL/kg body weight per day. Children weighing over 10 kg: refer to general recommendations for parenteral vitamins and trace elements. Add to compatible parenteral nutrition admixtures; do not give undiluted.'),(453,692,'Capsule, 100 mg. Adult and paediatric lead and mercury poisoning: 10 mg/kg or 350 mg/m² orally every 8 hours for 5 days, then every 12 hours for an additional 14 days. The course may be repeated if necessary, usually after an interval of at least 2 weeks unless blood lead concentrations indicate earlier treatment.'),(454,693,'Injection, prepared from a non-radioactive exametazime kit for radiolabelling with 99mTc prior to administration; precursor radiopharmaceutical. Adjunct in SPECT imaging of altered regional cerebral perfusion in stroke and for preparation of 99mTc-radiolabelled leukocytes: 555–1110 MBq (15–30 mCi).'),(455,694,'Oral capsule, 3.7, 7.4 and 14.8 MBq (100, 200 and 400 µCi). Adult: evaluation of thyroid function and/or thyroid morphology, oral: 3.7–14.8 MBq (100–400 µCi). Paediatric safety and effectiveness not established.'),(456,695,'Oral capsule, 0.33, 0.61, 1.11, 2.03 and 3.7 MBq (9, 16.5, 30, 55 and 100 µCi/capsule). Adult: thyroid uptake studies, oral: 0.185–0.555 MBq (5–15 µCi); scintiscanning, oral: 1.85–3.7 MBq (50–100 µCi); localization of iodide-avid metastatic lesions, oral: 37 MBq (1000 µCi). Paediatric safety and effectiveness not established.'),(457,696,'Injection, prepared from a non-radioactive betiatide kit for radiolabelling with 99mTc. Adult renal imaging: IV 185–370 MBq (5–10 mCi). Paediatric: child over 1 month, IV 2.6–5.2 MBq/kg (70–140 µCi/kg), minimum 37 MBq (1 mCi).'),(458,697,'Injection, prepared from a non-radioactive medronate kit for radiolabeling with 99mTc. Adult skeletal scintigraphy: IV 740–1110 MBq (20–30 mCi). Paediatric skeletal scintigraphy: IV 9–11 MBq/kg (0.2–0.3 mCi/kg).'),(459,698,'Radionuclide generator, 1, 2, 2.5, 3, 4, 4.5, 5, 6, 7.5, 10, 12.5, 15, 18 or 20 Ci of 99Mo. Indication: source of sodium pertechnetate Tc-99m for preparation of approved 99mTc radiopharmaceuticals.'),(460,699,'Injection, prepared from a non-radioactive sestamibi kit for radiolabeling with 99mTc. Adult myocardial perfusion imaging: IV 370 MBq (rest) to 1110 MBq (stress) (10–30 mCi); breast imaging: 740–1110 MBq (20–30 mCi); parathyroid imaging: 740 MBq (20 mCi).'),(461,700,'Injection, prepared from a non-radioactive disofenin kit for radiolabeling with 99mTc. Adult hepatobiliary imaging: average 70-kg non-jaundiced patient 37–185 MBq (1–5 mCi); average 70-kg adult with serum bilirubin >5 mg/dL: 111–296 MBq (3–8 mCi). Paediatric safety and effectiveness not established.'),(462,701,'Injection, labelled leucocytes. Adult: localization of intraabdominal infection, inflammatory bowel disease, pulmonary infection and osteomyelitis, IV 185–370 MBq (5–10 mCi). Paediatric: 3.7–7.4 MBq/kg (0.1–0.2 mCi/kg), usual minimum 18–37 MBq; maximum should not exceed adult activity.'),(463,702,'Hepatobiliary imaging, IV: Average 70-kg non-jaundiced adult: 74–185 MBq (2–5 mCi). Average 70-kg adult with serum bilirubin >1.5 mg/dL: 111–370 MBq (3–10 mCi). Paediatric safety and effectiveness below 18 years have not been established.'),(464,703,'Brain imaging, IV: 370–740 MBq (10–20 mCi). Renal visualization and perfusion assessment, IV: 370–740 MBq (10–20 mCi). Renal visualization and GFR assessment, IV: 111–185 MBq (3–5 mCi). GFR estimation without renal imaging: 7.4–18.5 MBq (0.2–0.5 mCi). Lung ventilation aerosol inhalation: 925–1850 MBq (25–50 mCi), to achieve a lung dose of approximately 18.5–37 MBq (0.5–1 mCi). Paediatric renal visualization/perfusion: 3.7–7.4 MBq/kg, maximum 37–185 MBq; other uses as specified in the KNMF.'),(465,704,'Adult: Vesico-ureteral imaging, IV: 18.5–37 MBq (0.5–1 mCi); thyroid imaging, IV: 37–370 MBq (1–10 mCi); salivary gland imaging, IV: 37–185 MBq (1–5 mCi); nasolacrimal drainage imaging, IV: maximum 3.7 MBq (100 µCi). Paediatric: Vesico-ureteral imaging, IV: 18.5–37 MBq (0.5–1 mCi); thyroid imaging, IV: 2.22–2.96 MBq (60–80 µCi)/kg.'),(466,705,'Adult assessment of PSMA overexpression to assist evaluation of malignancy in patients with an existing diagnosis of prostate cancer: 3–5 MBq/kg. Injection concentration: 0.74–2.7 GBq/mL (20–100 mCi/mL).'),(467,706,'Adult IV: 5–10 mCi (185–370 MBq) for indicated oncology, cardiology and neurology imaging. Paediatric neurology, IV: 2.6 mCi. Injection concentration: 0.74–11.1 GBq (20–300 mCi/mL).'),(468,707,'Injection kit: 40 micrograms of oxodotreotide for radiolabeling with up to 1110 MBq (30 mCi) of [68Ga]GaCl2. Adult PET imaging for localization of somatostatin receptor-positive neuroendocrine tumors: IV 2 MBq/kg (0.054 mCi/kg), up to 200 MBq (5.4 mCi). Paediatric: follow adult dosing.'),(469,708,'Radionuclide generator, 0.74–1.85 GBq. Indication: elution of gallium-68 chloride solution for radiolabelling approved gallium-68 radiopharmaceuticals. Gallium-68 chloride solution should not be injected into patients.'),(470,710,'Adult benign prostatic hyperplasia: 5 mg orally daily. Review treatment at 3–6 months, then every 6–12 months.'),(471,711,'Adult benign prostatic hyperplasia: one 400 microgram capsule orally daily, after breakfast or the first meal of the day.'),(472,709,'Adult neuroendocrine somatostatin-positive gastroenteropancreatic tumours: 7.4 GBq (200 mCi) every 8 weeks, for a total of 4 doses. Injection: 370 MBq/mL (10 mCi/mL).'),(473,171,'Combination therapy: Co-trimoxazole (sulfamethoxazole + trimethoprim). Adults: Treatment of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, oral or IV infusion: sulfamethoxazole up to 100 mg/kg daily plus trimethoprim up to 20 mg/kg daily in 2–4 divided doses for 14–21 days. Prophylaxis: sulfamethoxazole 800 mg + trimethoprim 160 mg, or sulfamethoxazole 400 mg + trimethoprim 80 mg if the higher dose is not tolerated. Children: Treatment, oral or IV: trimethoprim 10 mg/kg every 12 hours for 14–21 days; alternatively, the total daily dose may be given in 3–4 divided doses. Prophylaxis, oral: trimethoprim 2.5–5 mg/kg; under 6 months: 20 mg once daily; 6 months–5 years: 40 mg once daily; 6–12 years: 80 mg once daily.'),(474,178,'Combination therapy: Rifapentine + isoniazid (3HP) for prevention of tuberculosis. Adults: 900 mg rifapentine + 900 mg isoniazid (three tablets) once weekly for 3 months. Children over 15 years: 900 mg rifapentine + 900 mg isoniazid (three tablets) once weekly for 3 months.'),(475,195,'Amoebiasis: Adults, asymptomatic carriers in non-endemic areas or eradication of residual luminal amoebae after treatment of invasive disease with other drugs: oral 500 mg three times daily for 10 days. Children over 25 kg: oral 7 mg/kg (maximum 500 mg) three times daily for 10 days; the course may be repeated if necessary.');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `dosages` ENABLE KEYS */;
UNLOCK TABLES;

--
-- Table structure for table `medicine_substances`
--

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `medicine_substances`;
/*!40101 SET @saved_cs_client     = @@character_set_client */;
/*!50503 SET character_set_client = utf8mb4 */;
CREATE TABLE `medicine_substances` (
  `medicine_id` int NOT NULL,
  `substance_id` int NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (`medicine_id`,`substance_id`),
